TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2): Space Mission Design","overview":"Develop a preliminary space-mission design that benefits Australia and report the mission concept and its space-segment elements. Use mission-design software of your choice, supported by hand calculations if useful, and justify the proposed design."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale. Include the following required information: What is the spacecraft's primary mission? Why is the mission needed? How many spacecraft are used and why? Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design. Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate. Include relevant screenshots and software files in the main body of the report and/or appendix. Include the following information: Where will it launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? Which ground control facilities will you use? How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (e.g. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R3","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R7","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R11","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A 600 km altitude with Earth radius 6371 km gives a semi-major axis of about 6971 km, not 7171 km. The listed semi-major axis therefore conflicts with the stated altitude."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"At approximately 600 km, a sun-synchronous orbit requires a retrograde inclination near 97.8°, not 87.79°. The stated inclination is prograde and cannot produce the claimed sun-synchronous nodal precession."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The stated RAAN does not follow from the launch-site location. RAAN depends on the selected orbital plane and epoch; launch-site latitude alone does not determine it."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Kourou is at approximately 5.2° N, not 45.05° N. The stated latitude is wrong, and launch-window availability cannot be inferred from comparing that latitude with the target inclination alone."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The reported mean access duration of 580.786 seconds is about 9.68 minutes, not almost twenty minutes. The report also gives no link budget or data-rate analysis to substantiate that contacts can transfer the collected imagery."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Sun-synchronous orbit gives a consistent local solar time, not constant sunlight. The claim that the satellite is constantly sunlit on its descending path is not demonstrated and is generally not assured."},{"where":"Introduction","issue":"No sensor specifications, spatial resolution, spectral bands or swath are given to show that an orbital payload can perform the proposed reef imaging or fluid-lensing analysis. The image-formation and underwater-visibility constraints are not assessed."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is identified, but the report does not establish that its radio astronomy facilities provide satellite TT&C capability, suitable antennas, frequencies or operational ground-control services."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"A drag sail is proposed without sizing, deployment details or an estimated decay time at the stated altitude. The report therefore does not demonstrate that the disposal plan meets a debris-mitigation lifetime."}],"overall":"The report is well structured and covers most requested mission segments, with useful STK outputs. However, fundamental orbit-element and sun-synchronous-orbit errors undermine the design, while payload feasibility, communications capability and EOL performance are insufficiently substantiated.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The mission purpose and Australian reef rationale are explained, and the choice of one spacecraft is justified. Scientists are identified as users, but the operating organisation and paying or operational customers are not clearly named. The mission concept also lacks detail on the payload and its suitability for the proposed observations.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"A near-real-time relay to monitoring stations on the ground would allow marine and conservation scientists to more accurately predict and react to coral bleaching events."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The ZYZZsat however, does not require such constant coverage as the reef bleaching events occur over days and weeks. Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":24.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch, orbit elements, nominal manoeuvres, ground access and EOL, and includes STK imagery and an access report. However, the central orbit calculation is incorrect, and several design claims are unsupported or inconsistent. No STK project files are supplied, and there is little payload, coverage, communications-link or disposal analysis. These significant errors and omissions limit the design evidence.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 87.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost twenty minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":4.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion summarises the selected launch, orbit and ground facility and relates them to the mission aim. It is mostly a recap rather than a detailed conclusion drawn from validated results; in particular, it repeats inaccurate claims about illumination and ground-station access.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has an executive summary, contents and recognisable engineering sections, but grammar and wording errors, inconsistent figure labelling/captions and incomplete reference formatting reduce professionalism. Wikipedia is used as a source despite the task excluding it, and the bibliography does not consistently provide full reference details.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite – specifically for coral reef and ocean monitoring."},{"where":"Launch","quote":"To compare existing spaceflight mission plans, a wiki sorted directory – ‘Timeline of spaceflight’ – will be referred to throughout the report [6]."},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
